# Fix Prompt for Failing Test frontend/integration/playwright:\<startup>

## Diagnosis

The Playwright test `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` navigates to `/`
and asserts a single `<h1>` reads exactly `Hello world`:

```ts
test('home page greets with Hello world', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  await expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world');
});
```

`tests/playwright.config.ts` boots the app with `docker compose up --build
frontend` (cwd = fixture root) and waits on `http://127.0.0.1:3000`.
`docker-compose.yaml` builds the `frontend` service from `./services/frontend`
and maps port `3000`. But **`services/frontend/` does not exist**, so there is
nothing to build or serve — the empty failure output reflects that the server
never came up.

`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares `services/frontend` as a
TypeScript **Next.js + React** service (App Router, server components by
default) and names `services/frontend/tsconfig.json` as the code quality gate.

**Fix:** create a minimal Next.js App Router service under `services/frontend/`
whose `/` route renders `<h1>Hello world</h1>`, built and served on port 3000 by
the existing Dockerfile-based compose service. All paths are under `services/*`
— every write is legal. Do NOT touch `docker-compose.yaml` (it already points at
`./services/frontend`, and it lives outside `services/*`), the test, or `docs/`.

## Target
Apply the changes under `services/*` only. The test at `tests/integration` and
the compose file are the contract; do NOT modify them.

